UPDATE articles SET body = '<p>When you shop for a car, the safety rating is often one of the first numbers you see. A car with five stars sounds safe, and a car with three stars sounds risky. That simple picture is partly true, but it leaves out a lot of important detail. A safety rating is a useful guide, not a promise, and understanding what sits behind the stars will help you make a much better decision.</p><h2>What a safety rating actually measures</h2><p>A safety rating is the result of a set of standard tests carried out by an independent testing organisation. The car is crashed in controlled ways, its safety equipment is checked, and the results are turned into a score. The best-known testing programmes publish their results publicly, so anyone can look up how a particular model performed.</p><p>The rating usually covers four areas: adult occupant protection, child occupant protection, pedestrian and cyclist protection, and safety assistance, which is the technology that helps the driver avoid a crash in the first place. A car cannot earn the top rating by doing well in crash tests alone, it needs good scores in all four areas.</p><h2>How crash tests work</h2><p>Crash tests reproduce the most common kinds of serious accident. In a frontal impact test, the car is driven into a barrier at a set speed, sometimes straight on and sometimes with only part of the front. In a side impact test, a moving barrier strikes the side of the car, and a pole test simulates sliding sideways into a tree or a lamp post.</p><p>Crash test dummies fitted with sensors sit inside the car. The sensors record the forces on the head, neck, chest and legs, and engineers use those readings to judge the risk of serious injury. The tests are carried out under the same conditions for every car, which is what makes the results comparable. But they are still a sample of real accidents, not a copy of them. Real crashes happen at many speeds and angles, so a test result tells you how a car behaves in a standard situation, not in every possible situation.</p><h2>Active safety: avoiding the crash</h2><p>Modern safety ratings give a lot of weight to systems that help prevent a crash. Automatic emergency braking can detect a vehicle, pedestrian or cyclist ahead and brake if the driver does not react in time. Lane keeping assistance warns the driver or gently steers the car back if it starts to drift out of its lane. Blind spot monitoring warns about vehicles the driver cannot see in the mirrors. Speed assistance and driver attention monitoring are also common.</p><p>These systems are helpful, but they have limits. Cameras and sensors can struggle in heavy rain, fog, snow or low sunlight, and a system may not detect every type of hazard. They are designed to support the driver, not to replace attention, judgement and safe driving.</p><h2>What five stars does not guarantee</h2><p>A five-star rating is a strong result, but it is not a guarantee that everyone will walk away from any crash. No car can protect its occupants in every accident. Speed, the size and weight of the other vehicle, the angle of impact and the condition of the road all matter. A small car with five stars can still be at a disadvantage in a crash with a much larger and heavier vehicle.</p><p>Ratings also change over time. Testing programmes raise their standards regularly, so a car that earned five stars several years ago was tested against easier requirements than a car tested today. A five-star rating from an older year is not the same as a five-star rating from the current year. The rating also applies to the version of the car that was tested, and safety equipment can vary between trim levels and markets.</p><h2>How to use ratings when you buy</h2><p>Start by shortlisting cars that meet your needs for space, budget and running costs, then check the safety rating of each one. Read the full test result, not just the stars, and look at the four area scores and the notes on child seat fitting if you carry children. When you inspect a car, check that the safety equipment listed in the test is actually fitted to it, and ask which assistance systems are standard and which are optional extras.</p><ul><li>Look up the official rating for the exact model and year, not a similar model.</li><li>Compare ratings only from the same testing year and programme.</li><li>Read the area scores for adults, children, pedestrians and safety assistance.</li><li>Check that the tested safety equipment is fitted to the car you are buying.</li><li>Remember that a rating is one factor among many, alongside condition, tyres, maintenance and how the car is driven.</li></ul><p>A safety rating is one of the best tools a buyer has, because it is independent, standardised and public. Used carefully, it helps you separate genuinely well-protected cars from ones that only look safe. But it works best as part of a wider judgement: the right car, properly maintained, with good tyres and a careful driver, is always safer than a five-star badge on its own.</p>' WHERE id = 11;
UPDATE articles SET body = '<p>Choosing a family car means balancing three things that often pull in different directions: space, safety and running costs. A big car gives you room but can cost more to fuel and insure. A small car is cheap to run but may feel cramped on a long trip with children, luggage and a pushchair. The right choice depends on how your family actually uses a car day to day, not on which model looks best in a showroom.</p><h2>Start with how you use the car</h2><p>Before comparing cars, write down a typical week. How many people travel together most often? How far is the school run, the commute and the weekend trip? Do you need to fit child seats, a pushchair, sports equipment or a dog? Do you park in tight streets or a small garage? The answers narrow the choice quickly.</p><h2>Space: more than the boot number</h2><p>Manufacturers quote boot space in litres, but that number alone can mislead. The shape of the boot matters as much as its size. A wide, square boot with a low loading lip is easier to use than a deep, narrow one with the same litre figure, and a high roofline gives more room for tall child seats.</p><p>Check the rear seats in person. Sit in the back with the front seats set for your normal driving position and see how much knee room and headroom is left. Try fitting your child seats and check whether three seats fit across the back if you need them to. Wide-opening rear doors make it much easier to lift a child in and out, especially in a tight parking space.</p><h2>Safety: what to look for in a family car</h2><p>Safety matters more when children travel in the car, and family cars are generally expected to do well in independent crash tests. Look up the official rating for the exact model and year, and read the child occupant score as well as the adult score. Check how the car performed with child seats fitted, and whether the test notes mention any fitting problems.</p><p>Look for child seat anchor points that are easy to reach and clearly marked. Also check driver assistance systems such as automatic emergency braking and blind spot monitoring, and make sure the ones you value are fitted to the version you are buying. Remember that a safety rating is one factor, not a guarantee: always check the official rating for the exact model and year, and keep in mind that careful driving, good tyres and regular maintenance matter just as much.</p><h2>Running costs: the price beyond the price</h2><p>The purchase price is only the start. Fuel or electricity, insurance, servicing, tyres and depreciation all add up over the years you own the car. A car that looks cheap to buy can be expensive to run.</p><p>Fuel economy depends heavily on how you drive. A car that returns good economy on a motorway may use much more fuel on short city trips, and the reverse is true for some hybrids, which are at their best in stop-start traffic. If you can charge at home, an electric car can have very low running costs for daily driving. Get insurance quotes before you buy, and ask what a typical service costs.</p><h2>Comparing the main types of family car</h2><p>Small hatchbacks are the cheapest to buy, fuel and insure, and the easiest to park. They suit families with one or two young children and modest luggage needs, but rear space and boot size can feel tight as children grow. Medium hatchbacks and small SUVs add rear legroom and a larger boot while staying manageable in size and cost.</p><p>Mid-size SUVs are popular with families because they offer a high driving position, wide rear seats and a big boot. Estate cars offer similar boot space with a lower roofline and often better fuel economy. People carriers give the most interior space and the most flexible seating, including seven seats in some models, but they are bigger and heavier. Electric family cars can be very cheap to run day to day, but weigh charging access and purchase price against those savings.</p><h2>A practical way to decide</h2><p>Shortlist two or three cars that fit your space needs and your budget. For each one, look up the official safety rating for the exact model and year, get an insurance quote, and work out a rough yearly running cost including fuel or electricity, servicing and tyres. Then test drive each car with your family and usual luggage.</p><ul><li>Measure space with your own family and equipment, not just the brochure figures.</li><li>Check the child occupant score and the child seat fitting notes in the official test.</li><li>Compare total running costs over a year, not just the purchase price.</li><li>Confirm which safety systems are standard on the version you would actually buy.</li><li>Test drive on roads like the ones you use every day, with passengers and luggage on board.</li></ul><p>There is no single best family car, only the best fit for your family at this stage of life. A car that is spacious enough, safe enough and affordable enough to run will serve you far better than a bigger or faster car that strains the budget or never quite fits the school run.</p>' WHERE id = 12;
UPDATE articles SET body = '<p>Artificial intelligence, or AI, is mentioned almost everywhere now: in news stories, in adverts, in workplaces and in schools. The word can sound mysterious, as if it describes a machine that thinks like a person. In reality, most of the AI people use every day is much more ordinary and much more useful than that.</p><h2>What AI actually is</h2><p>AI is a broad name for computer systems that can do tasks which usually need human judgement, such as recognising speech, understanding written language, spotting patterns in data or making a suggestion. A system does not need to be conscious or to understand things the way a person does. It needs to take in information, follow patterns it has learned, and produce a useful result.</p><p>Most modern AI is built with a method called machine learning. Instead of a programmer writing down every rule by hand, the system is shown many examples and learns the patterns in them. It helps to think of AI as a very fast pattern finder: good at sorting, searching, summarising, predicting and generating, but not good at knowing what is true, what is fair or what matters, because those are judgements, not patterns.</p><h2>How AI works in everyday tools</h2><p>You may already use AI several times a day without noticing it. When you type on a phone and it suggests the next word, that is a small language model predicting what usually comes next. When a music or video app recommends something you might like, it is comparing your past choices with the choices of people who liked similar things.</p><p>Voice assistants turn your speech into text, work out the most likely meaning of your words, and then carry out a matching action, such as setting a timer or looking up the weather. Navigation apps predict traffic by combining live reports with patterns from past journeys at the same time of day.</p><p>Chatbots and writing tools are the most visible form of AI today. They are trained on very large amounts of text, so they can produce fluent sentences, answer questions, summarise documents and draft emails. They do this by predicting the most likely next words, not by looking up facts in a book. That is why they can sound confident and still be wrong, and why anything important they produce should be checked.</p><h2>Where AI helps most</h2><p>AI is most helpful when the task is repetitive, large or time-consuming, and when a human can check the result. It can draft a first version of a letter, summarise a long report into a few paragraphs, pull out the key dates from a pile of documents, or turn a rough list of ideas into a structured outline.</p><p>It is also good at handling scale, sorting large amounts of material and flagging what needs attention. For many people the most practical use is as a starting point. A blank page is hard, a rough draft is easier to fix than to create from nothing. Used this way, AI saves time without taking over the thinking. You stay in charge of the facts, the tone and the final decision.</p><h2>Where AI does not help, or can mislead</h2><p>AI is not a reliable source of facts. A chatbot can invent details, dates, names and references that sound convincing but are not real. It can mix up two similar topics, repeat a common misunderstanding, or answer a question it does not understand with a confident-sounding guess. Never rely on an AI answer for medical, legal, financial or safety decisions without checking it against a trusted source.</p><p>AI also reflects the material it was trained on. If the examples contain bias, errors or gaps, the system can repeat them. AI cannot replace human judgement, experience or responsibility. It cannot know your situation or your values unless you tell it, and it cannot take responsibility for a decision. If a task needs care, accuracy or accountability, a person must stay involved.</p><h2>How to use AI well: a simple approach</h2><p>Start small. Pick one task that takes up time and try using an AI tool for it, such as drafting a message, summarising notes or organising ideas. Give the tool clear instructions: what you want, who it is for, how long it should be and what tone to use. The clearer your instruction, the better the result.</p><p>Always check the output. Read it as if a helpful colleague had drafted it: useful, but not necessarily right. Check names, dates, numbers and facts against a reliable source, and remove anything that does not sound like you.</p><ul><li>Use AI for drafting, summarising and organising, not as a source of facts.</li><li>Give clear instructions and ask for a second attempt if the first is not right.</li><li>Check names, dates, numbers and claims against a trusted source.</li><li>Keep private and sensitive information out of AI tools unless you know how it is handled.</li><li>Stay responsible for the final decision, especially when it affects other people.</li></ul><p>AI is a tool, like a calculator or a search engine. It is not a person, not an expert and not a replacement for thinking. Used with that understanding, it can save time, help you learn and make routine work easier.</p>' WHERE id = 13;
UPDATE articles SET body = '<p>Many AI tools advertise themselves as free, but the word free can mean very different things. Some tools are free with a generous daily limit, some are free only for a short trial, and some are free because they collect your data or show you adverts. This guide describes seven types of genuinely useful free AI tool, what each one does, and what to check before you rely on it.</p><h2>1. A writing helper</h2><p>A writing helper drafts, rewrites and polishes text. You give it a rough idea, a bullet list or a first draft, and it produces a clearer version. Check how much you can write each day on the free tier, whether the tool keeps a copy of your text, and whether you can use it without creating an account.</p><h2>2. A summariser</h2><p>A summariser turns long text into a short version. It can reduce a long article, a meeting transcript or a report to its main points, which saves time when you need to understand something quickly. Check whether there is a limit on the length of the document you can summarise, and how accurate the summary is on a document you already know well. Read the full text for anything that matters.</p><h2>3. A research helper</h2><p>A research helper helps you explore a topic and find useful sources. It can suggest questions to ask, outline the main ideas, and point you towards material worth reading. Check whether the tool shows its sources and how recent they are. Never treat a research helper as a source on its own: follow the links and read the original material.</p><h2>4. An image generator</h2><p>An image generator creates pictures from a written description. You describe what you want, such as a simple illustration for a presentation or a social media post, and the tool produces one or more images. Check how many images you can generate for free each day or month, what size and quality you get, and whether you are allowed to use the images commercially.</p><h2>5. A transcription tool</h2><p>A transcription tool turns speech into written text. You upload a recording of a meeting, a lecture or an interview, and the tool produces a transcript you can search, edit and share. Check how many minutes of audio you can transcribe for free, how accurate the transcript is for your accent and subject, and how the tool handles your recordings. Recordings often contain private conversations, so check whether the audio is stored, how long it is kept, and whether you can delete it.</p><h2>6. A spreadsheet and data helper</h2><p>A spreadsheet helper works with tables and numbers. It can explain a formula in plain language, suggest a formula for a task, clean up a messy list, or help you build a simple chart from your data. Check whether the free tier limits the size of the file or the number of requests, and whether you need to upload your data to use it. Always check the result against your own calculations before you rely on it.</p><h2>7. A translation and language helper</h2><p>A translation helper turns text from one language into another and can explain what a phrase means in context. This is useful for travel, study, work with international contacts, and everyday communication. Check whether the free version limits the amount of text, whether it works well for your language pair, and how it handles your text. For anything important, have a person who knows the language check the result.</p><h2>How to choose and use free tools safely</h2><p>Before you settle on a tool, try it on a small, unimportant task and judge it by the result. A good tool is one that saves you time without creating new problems. Read the free tier limits carefully: a daily limit, a monthly credit allowance and a trial period are three different things, and a tool that feels free in the first week may stop working when the trial ends.</p><p>Pay attention to privacy. Check what the tool collects, whether it keeps your input, and whether you can delete your data. Prefer tools that let you export your work in a common format, such as plain text or a standard document, so you are not locked into one service. If a tool asks for more access than it needs, or will not explain how it uses your data, choose another one.</p><ul><li>Try each tool on a small task before using it for real work.</li><li>Read the free tier limits: daily caps, monthly credits and trial periods are not the same.</li><li>Check privacy and data storage, especially for recordings and private documents.</li><li>Make sure you can export your work in a common format.</li><li>Always review AI output before you send, publish or act on it.</li></ul><p>The best free AI tools are the ones that quietly remove a repetitive step from your day: a first draft, a summary, a transcript, a translation. None of them replaces your judgement, and none of them is worth using if it costs you your privacy or your data. Pick one type, try it for a week, keep the ones that genuinely save time, and drop the rest.</p>' WHERE id = 14;
UPDATE articles SET body = '<p>Buying a new phone is a bigger decision than it used to be. Phones now last longer, cost more, and differ from each other in ways that are not obvious from a quick look at the shop shelf. The camera, the battery, the storage and the length of software support matter more than small differences in speed for most people.</p><h2>Camera: look beyond the megapixels</h2><p>Camera makers often advertise a high megapixel number, but that number alone tells you very little about photo quality. A phone camera is a system: the lens, the sensor, the software that processes the image, and the way the phone handles low light all matter. A phone with a modest megapixel count and good software can take better photos than a phone with a huge number and poor processing.</p><p>Think about how you actually take photos. If most of your pictures are of family, friends and everyday life, the main camera and the quality of its colours and skin tones matter most. If you photograph children or pets, fast focus and a good portrait mode matter. If you take photos indoors or at night, low-light performance matters far more than zoom range.</p><h2>Battery: capacity is only part of the story</h2><p>Battery life is one of the first things people notice about a new phone, and one of the first things to disappoint them a year later. The battery size, usually given in milliamp hours, is only part of the story. The screen, the processor and the software all affect how long a charge lasts.</p><p>Your own habits matter more than any single test. Streaming video, navigation, gaming and photography use far more power than messaging or reading. If you are often away from a charger, look for a phone that reviewers and owners report as lasting a full day with heavy use, not just one that passes a light-use test.</p><p>Charging matters too. Check whether the phone supports fast charging, wireless charging, or both, and whether a charger is included in the box.</p><h2>Storage: buy for the life of the phone</h2><p>Storage is the amount of space on the phone for apps, photos, videos and files. It is also one of the hardest things to fix later, because most modern phones do not have a slot for a memory card. A phone that feels roomy on the first day can feel cramped a year later once photos, videos, messages and app updates have accumulated.</p><p>Think about what you keep on your phone. Photos and videos take up the most space, especially if you record video in high quality. Large games, offline maps, downloaded music and messaging app media all add up. Check how much storage your current phone uses, then add room to grow.</p><p>As a rough guide, a light user who streams most media and backs up photos regularly may be comfortable with a smaller capacity, while someone who stores many photos, videos and games on the phone itself should choose a larger one. It is usually better to have some spare room.</p><h2>Software support: the hidden value</h2><p>Software support is the length of time the maker will keep updating the phone. These updates include new features, fixes for problems, and security updates that protect the phone from new threats.</p><p>Before you buy, find out how many years of operating system updates and security updates the maker promises for that exact model. A phone with long support will stay safer and more useful for longer, which often makes it better value even if it costs a little more at the start.</p><h2>What else matters</h2><p>The screen affects everything you do on a phone. The size is a personal choice: a larger screen is better for video and reading, while a smaller phone is easier to hold and carry. Hold the phone in your hand and try using it one-handed before you decide.</p><p>Build quality and durability matter if you keep a phone for years. Check the warranty, what it covers, and how easy it is to get the phone repaired where you live. A phone that is cheap to repair is often cheaper to own than one that must be replaced after a cracked screen.</p><h2>A simple buying checklist</h2><p>Start by deciding how long you want to keep the phone and how much you want to spend. Then shortlist two or three phones that fit, and compare them on the things that matter to you: camera quality in real conditions, battery life under heavy use, enough storage for your photos and apps, and the length of software support.</p><ul><li>Test the camera in ordinary light, not just in the shop display.</li><li>Check battery life under heavy use and whether fast charging is included.</li><li>Choose storage for the whole life of the phone, not just the first month.</li><li>Confirm how many years of operating system and security updates are promised.</li><li>Hold the phone, try it one-handed, and check repair and warranty terms.</li></ul><p>The best phone for you is not necessarily the newest or the most expensive one. It is the one whose camera suits the photos you take, whose battery lasts through your day, whose storage fits your life, and whose software will still be supported when you are still using it.</p>' WHERE id = 15;
